  "query_preview": "..The upper pattern this afternoon is defined by an\nupper-low centered just west of us in western Maricopa county, \nanother upper low off the coast of British Columbia, a neutrally\noriented amplified ridge of high pressure off the West Coast and\na strong upper-low west of the ridge (centered around 35N/150W) \nin the Eastern Pacific.\n\n\nThe latest GOES precipitable water imagery indicated a modest \namount of moisture associated with the upper- low this morning \nacross Arizona, with PWATs 0.60-0.75 inches west of Tucson, and \ndrier air to the east (PWATs between 0.30-0.50 inches). Although\nthe moisture is limited, there should be just enough \ninstability under the upper-low to result in a slight chance of \nshowers, possibly with a thunderstorm across north central Pima \nand southwest Pinal county this afternoon. In fact, as of 1 PM \nMST...there are a few weak showers that have developed in \nextreme southern Maricopa county. Satellite imagery early this \nafternoon indicated a band of low to mid-level cloud cover that \nextends from central Pinal county south into the Tohono O'odham \nNation along the SW periphery of the upper low. It is possible \nthat this band will result in a slight chance of a weak shower \nlater this afternoon as the upper low drifts east across the \nTucson Metro Area, but should really struggle in the drier \nairmass to produce measurable rainfall.\n\nOtherwise, the upper-low to our west eventually ejects northeast \nSunday as the upper-low to our NW moves south along the Pacific \nNorthwest coast. The movement of the upper low will be stunted\nby the upper ridge to it south. The ridge will flatten as this\nhappens, creating a zonal westerly flow across the Eastern \nPacific early this week. This is important, because it will\nallow a deep plume of subtropical moisture to move east under\nthe strong upper low during the upcoming work week. Models still\nhave some uncertainty on the exact strength, timing and track \nof the upper-low as it shears out into the zonal flow and tracks\neast into the Desert Southwest, but the general consensus is\nfor showers and thunderstorms to impact Southeast Arizona\nFriday and Saturday with a stronger trough passage Sunday. The \n24/19Z 24-hr pcpn percentiles (25th/50th/75th) for for KTUS are: \n\n\t\t  Percentile of 24-hr PCPN (inches)\nDay\t\t\t25th \t50th\t75th \n\nFriday\t   0.12in  0.37in  0.56in\nSaturday   0.07in  0.36in  0.53in\nSunday     0.16in  0.63in  1.21in\nMonday \t   0.00in  0.37in  0.50in \t\n\nIn any event, it looks like a ramp up in activity the end of the \nupcoming week into next weekend with heavy rainfall possible\nSunday.",
